From: postmarketOS rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: rhodep: wire the IPA's three NoC paths

Companion to patch 0044, which gives ipa_data_v4_11_sm6375 an "imem" path.
Without this the driver asks for three interconnects the node does not declare
and IPA fails to probe.

The three are copied from the vendor node, which is the only description of
this SoC's IPA that exists:

	blair.dtsi:3196, qcom,ipa@0x5800000
		interconnects = <&system_noc MASTER_IPA &bimc SLAVE_EBI>,
				<&system_noc MASTER_IPA &system_noc SLAVE_OCIMEM>,
				<&bimc MASTER_AMPSS_M0 &config_noc SLAVE_IPA_CFG>;
		interconnect-names = "ipa_to_ebi1", "ipa_to_imem", "appss_to_ipa";

renamed to the "memory"/"imem"/"config" the mainline driver looks for.

This supersedes the old 0029 in docs/interconnect-sm6375-wip/, which was
written when the IPA node was still disabled and wired only two of the three,
missing the one that matters.

Each specifier carries two cells, the node id and an RPM tag, because the
providers declare #interconnect-cells = <2>, exactly as sm6115.dtsi does it.
Getting that wrong does not fail loudly: fw_devlink walks the list with the
wrong stride, takes a node id for a phandle, and parks the device on whatever
it points at. The first version of this patch omitted the tags and the IPA sat
forever on "wait for supplier /opp-table-cpu6", which is simply where the id 20
(SLAVE_IPA_CFG) happened to land.

Note the ordering constraint: the IPA node now depends on the interconnect
provider (patch 0028), so with INTERCONNECT_QCOM_SM6375=m the IPA sits at
-EPROBE_DEFER until that module is loaded. That is deliberate for now — it
keeps both out of the boot so the pair can be loaded by hand and a bad guess
costs a reboot rather than a reflash.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts
@@ -13,6 +13,8 @@
 #define PMK8350_SID 6
 
 #include <dt-bindings/gpio/gpio.h>
+#include <dt-bindings/interconnect/qcom,rpm-icc.h>
+#include <dt-bindings/interconnect/qcom,sm6375.h>
 #include <dt-bindings/input/input.h>
 #include <dt-bindings/power/qcom-rpmpd.h>
 #include <dt-bindings/clock/qcom,dsi-phy-28nm.h>
@@ -1350,6 +1352,42 @@
 		clocks = <&rpmcc RPM_SMD_IPA_CLK>;
 		clock-names = "core";
 
+		/*
+		 * Three NoC paths, copied from the vendor node, which is the
+		 * only description of this SoC's IPA that exists:
+		 *
+		 *   blair.dtsi:3196, qcom,ipa@0x5800000
+		 *     interconnect-names = "ipa_to_ebi1", "ipa_to_imem",
+		 *                          "appss_to_ipa";
+		 *
+		 * renamed to what the mainline driver looks for. "imem" is the
+		 * one that matters and that the other IPA v4.11 SoCs do not
+		 * have: the modem keeps its filter and route tables in IMEM at
+		 * 0x0c123000 and the IPA reads them through its own context
+		 * bank, so with no path voted the SoC resets without a word a
+		 * few minutes after the modem registers. See patch 0044 and
+		 * HANDOFF-SESSION4.md session 14.
+		 *
+		 * Two cells per specifier, node id and RPM tag, because the
+		 * providers declare #interconnect-cells = <2> - the same shape
+		 * sm6115.dtsi uses. Writing one cell here does not fail
+		 * loudly: fw_devlink walks the list with the wrong stride,
+		 * reads a node id as if it were a phandle, and blocks this
+		 * device on whatever that number happens to point at. Here it
+		 * was SLAVE_IPA_CFG (20) landing on opp-table-cpu6:
+		 *
+		 *   5840000.ipa platform: wait for supplier /opp-table-cpu6
+		 */
+		interconnects = <&system_noc MASTER_IPA RPM_ALWAYS_TAG
+				 &bimc SLAVE_EBI RPM_ALWAYS_TAG>,
+				<&system_noc MASTER_IPA RPM_ALWAYS_TAG
+				 &system_noc SLAVE_OCIMEM RPM_ALWAYS_TAG>,
+				<&bimc MASTER_AMPSS_M0 RPM_ALWAYS_TAG
+				 &config_noc SLAVE_IPA_CFG RPM_ALWAYS_TAG>;
+		interconnect-names = "memory",
+				     "imem",
+				     "config";
+
 		qcom,smem-states = <&ipa_smp2p_out 0>,
 				   <&ipa_smp2p_out 1>;
 		qcom,smem-state-names = "ipa-clock-enabled-valid",
